<SiteLayout title="About — Osama Ibrahim" description="A little context about Osama Ibrahim, developer and writer.">
<section class="page-hero about-editorial"><div class="container about-grid"><div><small class="eyebrow">A LITTLE CONTEXT / NOT THE WHOLE STORY</small><h1>More interested in the <em>why</em> than the headline.</h1><p>I build web applications and write about the technologies I'm using. The rest is easier to discover through the work and the things I choose to spend time explaining.</p></div><img class="portrait" src="/assets/me.jpg" alt="Portrait of Osama Ibrahim"/></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="container about-story-grid"><aside><small class="eyebrow">01 / THE WORK</small><span>BUILD · LEARN · REVISIT</span></aside><div class="simple-content"><h2>I like making the complicated a little easier to use.</h2><p>My work has moved between frontend interfaces, backend services, progressive web apps, and systems that help people organise real work. I’m interested in the decisions underneath the interface: what the problem actually is, what the software needs to remember, and what happens when the happy path ends.</p><p>I tend to document as I go. Some of those notes become technical articles; some remain attached to a project. Writing helps me slow down an idea long enough to see where it holds up — and where it doesn't.</p></div></div></section>
<section class="section about-writing-band"><div class="container about-story-grid"><aside><small class="eyebrow">02 / THE NOTES</small><span>EARLY WRITING, STILL USEFUL</span></aside><div class="simple-content"><h2>Explaining something is another way of learning it.</h2><p>A lot of my published writing comes from that habit: comparing tools before choosing one, breaking a concept into smaller parts, or documenting a side project while the details are still fresh.</p><p>The archive includes early JavaScript lessons, Node.js and Docker explainers, and a few project notes. They are snapshots of what I was learning and thinking at the time, not a claim that the conversation ends there.</p><a class="read-link" href="/writing/">Spend some time in the writing archive ↗</a></div></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="container about-story-grid"><aside><small class="eyebrow">03 / STILL IN PROGRESS</small><span>NO FINAL VERSION</span></aside><div class="simple-content"><h2>Curiosity is a moving target.</h2><p>I'm still building, revising, and learning in public where it makes sense. Some projects are active; others are historical work I can now look at with a little more perspective. I prefer to be clear about the difference.</p><p>And every so often, the best next step is to close the editor and be a person for a while.</p></div></div></section>
</SiteLayout>
